-- title:
--   Radical special fibre of a Drinfeld chart ring
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, and let $W$ be a complete discrete valuation ring: a commutative domain that is a discrete valuation ring and is adically complete for its maximal ideal. Let $\pi \in W$ generate the maximal ideal, i.e. $\mathfrak m_W = (\pi)$, and assume $q \in \mathfrak m_W$, so that the residue characteristic is $q$. Let $c \in \mathfrak m_W$ be nonzero, and let $f, u, v$ be elements of the ring $W[[X_0, X_1]]$ of formal power series in two variables indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,2$, with $u$ and $v$ units. Assume that $f$ agrees with the Drinfeld form $X_0 X_1^{q} - X_0^{q} X_1$ modulo the $(q+2)$nd power of the ideal $(X_0, X_1)$, that is, $f - (X_0 X_1^{q} - X_0^{q} X_1) \in (X_0, X_1)^{q+2}$. Then, in the quotient ring $W[[X_0,X_1]]/(c\,v - f\,u)$ (where $c$ denotes the constant power series with value $c$), the principal ideal generated by the image of the constant power series $\pi$ is a radical ideal.
--
--   This is the statement that the special fibre of a Drinfeld chart ring is reduced: modulo $\pi$ the equation $c\,v = f\,u$ degenerates to $f\,u \equiv 0$, whose reduction is a unit times a product of $q+1$ pairwise non-associated prime elements of $\kappa[[X_0,X_1]]$ corresponding to the distinct tangent lines of the Drinfeld form. It is used in the study of the completed local rings of a full-level modular curve model at supersingular points, where reducedness of the special fibre is what allows branch primes to be identified with primes extended from the base.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart_isRadical_span_C_of_sub_drinfeldForm_mem_pow.lean

